update public.moo_versions set body_md = body_md || E'| **Indicator**                 | **Suggested measure / evidence**                                                                 | **Review frequency**            |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| Plantilla compliance          | Filled required positions / required positions, with separate authorized and vacant counts.      | Quarterly / annual              |\n| Position-description coverage | Positions with current approved descriptions / total approved positions.                         | Annual                          |\n| Competency coverage           | Personnel meetings required role competencies / personnel assigned to those roles.               | Semi-annual                     |\n| Training plan implementation  | Completed priority training activities / approved priority activities, with learning evaluation. | Quarterly                       |\n| Duty coverage                 | Critical duty periods covered by designated primary or alternate personnel.                      | Monthly                         |\n| EOC roster depth              | Key EOC roles with primary and alternate personnel formally designated and available.            | Quarterly                       |\n| Exercise performance          | Corrective actions from exercises completed and verified by the due date.                        | After each exercise / quarterly |\n| Welfare and safety            | Incidents, near misses, PPE compliance, welfare actions and referrals, reported in aggregate.    | Quarterly                       |\n| Records completeness          | Required workforce and operational designation records complete and current.                     | Quarterly                       |\n\nTargets shall be set only after baseline validation and alignment with City performance management and budget processes. The MOO shall not create performance targets that conflict with national standards or approved City commitments.\n\n## 17.2 Controlled records\n\n- Approved organizational chart and organizational issuance register.\n\n- Approved plantilla, vacancy and staffing gap register.\n\n- Position descriptions and delegation / designation records.\n\n- Workforce and competency matrix.\n\n- Annual workforce plan and training needs assessment.\n\n- Training plan, attendance, certificates and evaluation records.\n\n- Duty rosters, alternates, handover and contact registers.\n\n- EOC personnel designation and liaison register.\n\n- Personnel safety, welfare and incident records, subject to restricted access.\n\n- Performance monitoring and corrective action records.\n\n# 18. Priority Institutional Improvement Actions\n\nThe October 2026 assessment (IAT v1.7) shows that the CDRRMO''s organizational and staffing arrangements need strengthening. The actions below follow the workbook''s gap-priority order and its Action Plan targets. They are proposed for validation and do not replace HRMO, Budget, DBM, CSC or City Legal Office approvals.\n\n![Figure 18: 18. Priority Institutional Improvement Actions](/library/ch2v12/fig-18.webp)\n\n**Table 2.6**\n\n*Priority Institutional Improvement Actions for Organization and Staffing*\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 8%" />\n<col style="width: 11%" />\n<col style="width: 33%" />\n<col style="width: 20%" />\n<col style="width: 17%" />\n<col style="width: 8%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Priority</strong></th>\n<th><strong>IAT ref (score)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Priority action</strong></th>\n<th><p><strong>Lead owner</strong></p>\n<p><strong>supporting actors</strong></p></th>\n<th><strong>Required output / evidence</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Target</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A2.1 (2)</td>\n<td>Validate the structure against JMC 2014-1 and current City issuances; approve the functional organogram through the A1.1 ordinance.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>HRMO; CLO; Budget</p></td>\n<td>Approved functional organogram</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A2.3 (2)</td>\n<td>Reconcile the plantilla against minimum staffing; request the 10 planned items in the FY2028 budget, first for Operations and Warning (OpCen to at least 9) and Research and Planning (at least 4 technical posts).</td>\n<td><p>HRMO; CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>Budget</p></td>\n<td>Position-by-position matrix; budget proposal</td>\n<td>FY2028 budget</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A2.5 / B4.3 (2)</td>\n<td>Approve the OpCen SOP; split tasks across the three monitoring streams; keep rosters and handovers.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Operations and Warning</p>\n<p>HRMO</p></td>\n<td>Approved SOP; 3 months of rosters and signed handovers</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>A2.9 (0)</td>\n<td>Designate the anticipatory-action focal person by office order; enrol them in AA training.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>OCD VIII; BDRRMCs; ACDVs; CSOs</p></td>\n<td>Office order; training records</td>\n<td>Dec 2026</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>High</td>\n<td>B4.1 / B4.2 (1)</td>\n<td>Define the EOC organogram; name a primary and two alternates for each key position; review quarterly.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>Participating offices; HRMO; OCD</p></td>\n<td>EOC designation register (Annex 2-D)</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>A2.4 (2)</td>\n<td>Write position descriptions for every CDRRMO and EOC role; link them to IPCR/OPCR targets.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Admin and Training; HRMO</p>\n<p>Division chiefs</p></td>\n<td>Approved position descriptions (Annex 2-A)</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>A2.6 (1)</td>\n<td>Run a training needs analysis; produce a 3-year training plan with OCD VIII courses.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Admin and Training</p>\n<p>HRMO; OCD VIII</p></td>\n<td>Competency matrix; funded plan (Annex 2-C)</td>\n<td>Q1 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>A2.7 (2)</td>\n<td>Adopt a responder welfare policy: group accident insurance for staff and ACDVs, PPE issue list, rest cycles, MHPSS referral with the CHO.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO; HRMO; CHO</p>\n<p>Budget</p></td>\n<td>Approved policy; insurance policy on file</td>\n<td>FY2027 AIP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>B4.4 / B4.5 (2 / 1)</td>\n<td>List agency liaison desks with focal persons; write job action sheets for every EOC role.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO</p>\n<p>CSWDO, CHO, CEO, PNP, BFP, DepEd, utilities, Red Cross</p></td>\n<td>Liaison register; job action sheets</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>B5.2 / A7.3 (1–2)</td>\n<td>Pilot cluster-based barangay DRRM coordination in two clusters for the June–November 2027 season; scale through the FY2028 budget if the measures are met (section 10.2).</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>Research and Planning; Liga; BDRRMCs</p></td>\n<td>Cluster map; pilot report against 5 measures</td>\n<td>Jun–Nov 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>A4.4 / A10.4 (2)</td>\n<td>Adopt the budget-cycle RACI (2.16.2) and a quarterly results-and-finance report.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>All divisions; Budget Office</p></td>\n<td>Approved RACI; quarterly reports</td>\n<td>Dec 2026 workshop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Institutionalize the annual workforce and capability review.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>HRMO; Budget; divisions</p></td>\n<td>Annual review report; corrective action register</td>\n<td>Yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A2.2 (3)</td>\n<td>Complete the qualification records (CSC QS) and the OIC order of precedence for the head-of-office post.</td>\n<td><p>HRMO; CDRRMO Head</p>\n<p>CLO</p></td>\n<td>Verified records; succession order</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A2.8 (3)</td>\n<td>File the signed MOA; keep the ACDV registry; route tasking through the EOC; add AA tasks.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Admin and Training</p>\n<p>Partner CSOs</p></td>\n<td>Registry; signed MOA</td>\n<td>Q2 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>A2.10 (1)</td>\n<td>Build a skills inventory and update it every 12 months.</td>\n<td><p>CDRRMO Admin and Training</p>\n<p>CDRRMC offices; BDRRMCs</p></td>\n<td>Skills inventory</td>\n<td>Q1 2027</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class="even">\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>B4.6 (1)</td>\n<td>Write rest cycles, meals and MHPSS for EOC staff.</td>\n<td>CDRRMO; CHO</td>\n<td>EOC welfare provisions</td>\n<td>Jan 2027</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n*Note.* Targets follow the IAT v1.7 Action Plan and remain subject to HRMO, Budget, DBM, CSC and City Legal Office approvals.\n\n# 19. Required SOPs, Forms and Controlled Records\n\nThe following instruments shall be maintained as controlled annexes to the MOO or as linked City HR and administrative documents. Where a subject is already governed by an approved City-wide HR policy, the CDRRMO shall cross-reference that policy rather than duplicate or contradict it.\n\n| **Code**   | **Instrument / template**                              | **Purpose / minimum contents**                                                                    |\n|------------|--------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| ORG-SOP-01 | CDRRMO Workforce Planning and Staffing Review          | Annual review cycle; data requirements; roles; approval route; action tracking.                   |\n| ORG-SOP-02 | Position Description and Delegation Control            | Preparation, review, approval, version control and delegation register.                           |\n| ORG-SOP-03 | Duty Roster, Handover and Continuity                   | Primary/alternate roster; escalation; shift handover; absence and succession arrangements.        |\n| ORG-SOP-04 | Competency Assessment and Training Management          | Competency profiles; training needs assessment; annual plan; evaluation and records.              |\n| ORG-SOP-05 | Personnel Safety and Welfare Coordination              | Risk assessment; PPE; medical and psychosocial support; fatigue; incident reporting and referral. |\n| ORG-F01    | Organizational Structure and Position Register         | Approved division, position, item number, reporting line, status and authority.                   |\n| ORG-F02    | Workforce and Vacancy Matrix                           | Required, authorized, filled, vacant, unavailable and proposed positions.                         |\n| ORG-F03    | Position Description Template                          | Purpose, duties, qualifications, competencies, authority, interfaces and performance.             |\n| ORG-F04    | Delegation and Designation Register                    | Delegating authority, delegate, scope, duration, conditions and reporting.                        |\n| ORG-F05    | Competency Matrix                                      | Position, required competency, proficiency level, evidence and gap.                               |\n| ORG-F06    | Training Needs Assessment and Annual Plan              | Gap, priority, target personnel, provider, cost, schedule and evaluation.                         |\n| ORG-F07    | Duty Roster and Handover Form                          | Duty period, primary, alternate, open issues, warnings, incidents and equipment status.           |\n| ORG-F08    | EOC Primary and Alternate Personnel Register           | EOC role, office, primary, alternate, contact, competency and designation.                        |\n| ORG-F09    | Personnel Safety / Welfare Incident Form               | Incident type, immediate action, referral and restricted record controls.                         |\n| ORG-F10    | Annual Workforce Review and Corrective Action Register | Findings, root causes, actions, owner, deadline, evidence and closure.                            |\n\n# 20. Implementation, Review and Chapter Conclusion\n\nThe CDRRMO Head shall lead implementation of this chapter in coordination with HRMO, Budget, the City Legal Office and the relevant City offices. The first implementation cycle shall establish the verified organizational baseline: current legal basis, approved organogram, plantilla, filled and vacant positions, qualification status, position descriptions, duty arrangements, competency profile and welfare controls.\n\nImplementation shall proceed through approved City processes. No provision of this chapter shall be interpreted as independently authorizing the creation of positions, appointment of personnel, payment of additional compensation, transfer of personnel, procurement of equipment or expenditure of public funds.\n\nThis chapter shall be reviewed at least annually and whenever there is a material change in law, national guidance, City organizational structure, staffing pattern, risk profile, operational requirements or institutional assessment findings. Revisions shall be controlled, approved by the competent authority and communicated to all affected personnel.\n\nA mature CDRRMO is not defined merely by its formal existence. It is defined by its ability to sustain qualified personnel, clear functional responsibilities, reliable duty coverage, institutional memory, safe working conditions and coordinated performance across normal operations and emergencies. By linking organizational design to workforce planning, competency development, operational readiness and continuous improvement, this chapter provides the human and institutional capacity foundation for the succeeding operational modules.\n\n# 21. Integrated Process Architecture and Process Flows\n\nTo operationalize the organizational model, the CDRRMO shall manage workforce and institutional-support activities as controlled end-to-end processes. Each process must identify its trigger, accountable owner, authorized decision-maker, required inputs, control points, outputs, records, service standard, escalation path and review mechanism. The flows below describe the normal administrative pathway; urgent life-safety action during an incident shall follow the applicable emergency procedures and shall not be delayed by routine HR approval steps.\n\n## 21.1 End-to-end Workforce Management Cycle\n\n![Figure 19: 21.1 End-to-end Workforce Management Cycle](/library/ch2v12/fig-19.webp)\n\nA**nnual cycle**: City DRRM priorities and approved plans → workload and capability analysis → review of approved structure and plantilla → identify critical gaps and risks → prepare staffing, succession and learning proposals → HRMO/Finance/legal review → approval by competent authority → recruitment or lawful designation → induction and competency development → performance and welfare monitoring → annual evaluation and corrective action. Any proposal requiring a new or reclassified position, compensation adjustment or additional appropriation must pass the applicable City, CSC and DBM processes before implementation.\n\n| Stage                | Process owner / lead                      | Control / decision gate                                                           | Required evidence                                      |\n|----------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Analyze demand   | CDRRMO Head with division leads           | Link tasks to LDRRMP, risk profile, service standards and workload                | Workload analysis; risk and service demand register    |\n| 2\\. Assess supply    | HRMO with CDRRMO                          | Verify authorized plantilla, filled/vacant posts, qualifications and availability | Certified plantilla; staffing and competency inventory |\n| 3\\. Identify gap     | CDRRMO Head                               | Separate structural, staffing, skill, equipment and process gaps                  | Gap analysis; risk rating; options appraisal           |\n| 4\\. Develop proposal | CDRRMO / HRMO                             | No assumed position creation; assess alternatives and cost                        | Staffing plan; draft PDFs; cost and funding note       |\n| 5\\. Review / approve | HRMO, Budget, CLO; Mayor/SP as applicable | Approval follows statutory and administrative authority                           | Review endorsements; approved issuance / appropriation |\n| 6\\. Implement        | HRMO and CDRRMO                           | Appointment/designation and onboarding comply with rules                          | Appointment; designation; induction record             |\n| 7\\. Verify / improve | CDRRMO Head / HRMO                        | Test service coverage, competence, safety and outcomes                            | KPI dashboard; CAPA; annual review                     |\n\n## 21.2 Process flow – Organizational and Plantilla Change\n\n![Figure 20: 21.2 Process flow – Organizational and Plantilla Change](/library/ch2v12/fig-20.webp)\n\n**Trigger**: new mandate, material workload change, assessment finding, vacancy pattern or approved restructuring priority.  \nCDRRMO documents need → HRMO validates organizational and position requirements → Budget assesses fiscal space and recurring cost → City Legal reviews legal authority and instrument → competent authority approves or rejects → HRMO processes authorized staffing action → CDRRMO updates organogram, role assignments and MOO → implementation is monitored and reviewed.  \nHOLD POINT: no position may be treated as created, funded or fillable until the required approval, appropriation and plantilla action are complete.\n\n## 21.3 Process flow – Recruitment, Appointment and Onboarding Interface\n\n![Figure 21: 21.3 Process flow – Recruitment, Appointment and Onboarding Interface](/library/ch2v12/fig-21.webp)\n\nVacancy / approved staffing need confirmed → HRMO verifies authorized vacant plantilla and qualification standards → CDRRMO validates technical competency profile → HRMO applies approved Merit Selection Plan and recruitment procedures → selection and recommendation → appointing authority acts → HRMO completes appointment documentation and assumption → CDRRMO conducts role-specific induction and safety briefing → supervisor sets performance outputs and learning plan → probationary / periodic performance review as applicable. CDRRMO does not conduct appointments or waive civil-service requirements.\n\n## 21.4 Process flow – Competency and Learning Cycle\n\n![Figure 22: 21.4 Process flow – Competency and Learning Cycle](/library/ch2v12/fig-22.webp)\n\nPosition competency profile → individual competency assessment → consolidate competency gaps → prioritize by life-safety, statutory, operational and service risk → approve annual learning plan and resources → deliver training / coaching / exercise → assess demonstrated competence (not attendance alone) → update individual records and duty eligibility → reassess and close gaps. Critical assignments must be limited to personnel who meet the approved competency and safety prerequisites, with supervised development pathways for trainees.\n\n**21.5 Process flow – duty roster, handover and operational augmentation**\n\n![Figure 23: 21.5 Process flow – duty roster, handover and operational augmentation](/library/ch2v12/fig-23.webp)\n\nForecast duty demand and availability → supervisor prepares roster from trained and available personnel → division head verifies coverage, alternates and fatigue controls → CDRRMO Head approves roster under delegated authority → personnel acknowledge assignment → outgoing/incoming duty officers complete structured handover → exceptions or unfilled shifts escalated immediately → EOC activation or surge request is processed under the relevant EOC chapter → duty records reconciled after shift → gaps entered into CAPA. This chapter establishes personnel readiness; activation levels, EOC configuration and incident command remain in Chapters 4 and 6.\n\n## 21.6 Process flow – Personnel Safety, Welfare and Incident Reporting\n\n![Figure 24: 21.6 Process flow – Personnel Safety, Welfare and Incident Reporting](/library/ch2v12/fig-24.webp)\n\nUnsafe condition / injury / exposure / fatigue / psychosocial concern identified → make area safe and obtain immediate medical or protective assistance → notify supervisor and designated safety focal person → preserve incident facts and protect privacy → refer to HRMO / OSH / health provider / MHPSS provider as appropriate → assess fitness for duty and accommodation → investigate systemic causes without prejudging personnel accountability → implement corrective action → verify effectiveness and close record. Emergency response and medical care take precedence over administrative reporting.\n\n## 21.7 Process flow – Performance Management and Corrective Action\n\n![Figure 25: 21.7 Process flow – Performance Management and Corrective Action](/library/ch2v12/fig-25.webp)\n\nApproved office and individual outputs → periodic coaching and evidence capture → performance review under the City''s approved SPMS → identify performance or process gap → distinguish individual competency issue from systemic constraint → agree improvement action and support → monitor milestones → evaluate results → recognize good performance or initiate appropriate administrative process where warranted → feed systemic lessons into workforce planning and MOO revision.\n\n# 22. Standard Operating Procedures and Protocols\n\nThe following SOPs are proposed as controlled annexes to this chapter. They are operational templates and shall take effect only after review and approval under City procedures. They supplement, and do not supersede, HRMO policies, the City''s approved Merit Selection Plan, SPMS, records retention schedule, occupational safety procedures or applicable civil-service rules.\n\n## 22.1 Chapter 2 Master RACI\n\n| Process / output                          | CDRRMO Head | Division lead / supervisor | HRMO               | Budget / Accounting | CLO | Mayor / SP / appointing authority         | DPO / OSH / Health       |\n|-------------------------------------------|-------------|----------------------------|--------------------|---------------------|-----|-------------------------------------------|--------------------------|\n| Annual workforce plan                     | A           | R                          | R/C                | C                   | C   | I / A where approval required             | C                        |\n| Organizational / plantilla proposal       | R           | C                          | A/R                | C                   | C   | A where legally required                  | I                        |\n| Position description / competency profile | A           | R                          | R/C                | I                   | C   | I                                         | C                        |\n| Recruitment / selection administration    | C           | C                          | A/R                | I                   | C   | A for appointment                         | I                        |\n| Onboarding and role induction             | A           | R                          | R                  | I                   | I   | I                                         | C                        |\n| Training needs analysis / L&D plan        | A           | R                          | C                  | C                   | I   | I / A for resources                       | C                        |\n| Duty roster and handover                  | A           | R                          | I                  | I                   | I   | I                                         | C                        |\n| EOC personnel designation register        | A           | R                          | C                  | I                   | C   | A if designation requires Mayor authority | C                        |\n| Personnel safety / welfare response       | A           | R                          | R/C                | C                   | C   | I                                         | R/C                      |\n| Performance management                    | A           | R                          | R/C                | I                   | I   | I                                         | C                        |\n| Personnel record access / sharing         | A           | R                          | A/R for HR records | I                   | C   | I                                         | A/R for privacy controls |\n| CAPA and annual review                    | A           | R                          | C                  | C                   | C   | I / A for policy changes                  | C                        |\n\nRACI interpretation: one Accountable (A) role is assigned per activity, except where a collective statutory or formal approval authority must be named explicitly. In such cases the matrix distinguishes process accountability from the legal approving authority. HRMO remains the custodian and authority for official personnel administration; the CDRRMO owns operational requirements and role performance. The RACI matrix does not transfer appointing, disciplinary, budgetary or statutory powers.\n\n## 22.2 SOP register and minimum controls\n\n| SOP code | Title                                                       | Process owner                  | Minimum control / record                                                           |\n|----------|-------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| HR-01    | Annual workforce planning and plantilla review              | CDRRMO Head / HRMO             | Workload analysis, certified plantilla, gap matrix, approval trail                 |\n| HR-02    | Position description and competency profile management      | HRMO / CDRRMO Head             | Approved PDF, competency profile, version register                                 |\n| HR-03    | Recruitment, appointment interface and onboarding           | HRMO                           | Vacancy verification, selection records, appointment, induction checklist          |\n| HR-04    | Competency assessment and learning & development            | CDRRMO Head / HRMO             | TNA, competency assessment, annual L&D plan, evaluation and qualification register |\n| HR-05    | Duty roster, shift handover and personnel availability      | CDRRMO Head / Division leads   | Approved roster, attendance, handover, exception and fatigue log                   |\n| HR-06    | EOC personnel designation and surge augmentation            | CDRRMO Head / EOC Manager      | Designation register, alternates, competency and availability checks               |\n| HR-07    | Personnel safety, welfare, injury and psychosocial referral | CDRRMO Head / HRMO / OSH focal | Risk assessment, PPE, incident report, referral and corrective action              |\n| HR-08    | Performance management, records and CAPA                    | CDRRMO Head / HRMO             | IPCR/OPCR evidence, coaching notes, CAPA, records and review log                   |\n\n# REFERENCES\n\n9. City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). *CDRRMO reference: Local policies* [Shared Drive folder of the City''s DRRM policy instruments: Sangguniang Panlungsod ordinances, executive orders, CDRRMC resolutions and SP resolutions]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12NWwUTAE6ub1vW31M2NSMktYlP-BP4oR\n\n10. City Government of Ormoc, Sangguniang Panlungsod. (1993–2025). *SP ordinances* [Shared Drive folder, by year, including Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 establishing the CDRRMO and Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015 amending it]. https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/132g-VeSpXkorcNCte2fpyVY3Az3baBXS\n\n11.  City Government of Ormoc. (2026). Inception report: Ormoc City CDRRMO and EOC Manual of Operations development. Unpublished project document.\n\n12. City Government of Ormoc. (2026). Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (v1-7, 11 November 2026). Unpublished assessment workbook.\n\n13. City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2022). Resolution Nos. 03 and 04, s. 2022: Adoption of NDRRMC Memorandum Circular No. 64, s. 2021 and risk categorization for ACDV insurance.\n\n14. City Government of Ormoc, Office of the City Mayor. (2022). Executive Order Nos. 2022-0006 (CDRRMC reorganization) and 2022-00020 (CDRRMP Technical Working Group), and Executive Order on the City CCCM Team, s. 2022.\n\n15. Sangguniang Panlungsod ng Ormoc. (2023). Resolution No. 2023-347: MOA with CPR-RIMT Rapid Rescue Rider Ormoc Chapter.\n\n16. City Government of Ormoc, City Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office. (2026). CDRRMO organizational chart and internal staff papers on planning, budgeting and cluster-based barangay coordination. Unpublished working papers.\n\n17. City Government of Ormoc, Office of the City Mayor. (2023). Executive Order No. 2023-0085: BDRRMP Review Team.\n\n18. City Government of Ormoc. (2026). Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Standards and Policy Methodology. Unpublished project document.\n\n19. Republic Act No. 7160. (1991). Local Government Code of 1991.\n\n20. Republic Act No. 10121. (2010). Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010.\n\n21. Republic Act No. 10173. (2012). Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\n22. Republic Act No. 11036. (2018). Mental Health Act.\n\n23. Republic Act No. 12287. (2025). Declaration of State of Imminent Disaster Act.\n\n24. Republic Act No. 9710. (2009). Magna Carta of Women.\n\n25. Republic Act No. 7277, as amended. Magna Carta for Persons with Disability.\n\n26. Republic Act No. 9994. (2010). Expanded Senior Citizens Act of 2010.\n\n27. Republic Act No. 10821. (2016). Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act.\n\n28. National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council, Department of the Interior and Local Government, Department of Budget and Management, & Civil Service Commission. (2014). Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1: Guidelines on the establishment of local disaster risk reduction and management offices (LDRRMOs) and staffing pattern.\n\n29. National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2012). Memorandum Circular No. 4, s. 2012: Institutionalization of the Incident Command System (ICS) in the Philippines. \\[Verify exact title and current status\\].\n\n30. Office of Civil Defense / National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (n.d.). Emergency Operations Center guidelines. \\[Exact issuance number, title and current status to be verified with OCD Region VIII\\].\n\n31. United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\n32. Civil Service Commission. (2026). CSC Resolution No. 2600005: Guidelines on the Development of Agency Competency-Based Human Resource Management System. https://www.csc.gov.ph/downloads/category/562-csc-reso-2600005\n\n33. Republic Act No. 11058. (2018). An Act Strengthening Compliance with Occupational Safety and Health Standards and Providing Penalties for Violations Thereof.\n\n34. National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council, Department of the Interior and Local Government, Department of Budget and Management, & Civil Service Commission. (2014). Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1: Implementing guidelines for the establishment of Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Offices or Barangay Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Committees in Local Government Units. https://www.dbm.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/Issuances/2014/Joint%20Memorandum%20Circular/JMC2014-1%20_LDRRMOs.pdf\n\n**Reference control note**: Exact titles, amendments, current effectivity and the official versions of administrative issuances shall be verified by the City Legal Office and OCD Region VIII before final approval. This chapter does not rely on unverified summaries as a substitute for certified legal texts.\n'
